  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 364,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 164,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56390,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 243,
        "headingId": 164,
        "headingText": "<p></p>\r\n<p>About 95% of patients with PJS have polyps in the small intestine. A reported 25% of patients have polyps in the colon and stomach {26721304}, but this may be an underestimate, because polyps in these sites are not as likely as small bowel polyps to be clinically prominent.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-01-16T16:13:52"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 365,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 165,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56391,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 243,
        "headingId": 165,
        "headingText": "<p>The diagnostic criteria are summarized in Box&nbsp;<a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/attachment/31/243/0054\" target=\"_blank\">#0054</a>. Presenting symptoms include abdominal pain, intestinal bleeding, anaemia, and intussusception, which typically manifest in the first two decades of life {17311531}. If present, the characteristic mucocutaneous pigmentation facilitates diagnosis of asymptomatic patients in familial cases, but the characteristic Peutz&ndash;Jeghers polyps are the main clinical hallmark. PJS is associated with a moderate or high risk of a range of malignancies, with an overall risk of any cancer by the age of 70&nbsp;years of 81% (see Table&nbsp;<a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/attachment/31/243/0055\" target=\"_blank\">#0055</a>) {16707622; 11113065; 3587280}. Well-documented extraintestinal tumours include carcinomas of the breast and pancreas, as well as otherwise rare gonadal lesions, including sex cord tumour with annular tubules of the ovary and Sertoli cell tumour of the testis.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-08-23T14:19:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 366,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 166,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56392,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 243,
        "headingId": 166,
        "headingText": "<p></p>\r\n<p>The incidence of PJS is roughly one tenth that of FAP, with an estimated incidence of 1&nbsp;case per 50&nbsp;000&ndash;200&nbsp;000 births.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-01-16T16:14:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 367,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 167,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56393,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 243,
        "headingId": 167,
        "headingText": "<p>A germline mutation in the tumour suppressor gene <em>STK11</em> (formerly called <em>LKB1</em>), which encodes a serine/threonine kinase, can be found in &gt;&nbsp;90% of patients with PJS. Most germline defects are point mutations and small intragenic deletions, but some larger deletions of one or more exons have also been described {17924967}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-01-16T16:14:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 368,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 168,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56394,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 243,
        "headingId": 168,
        "headingText": "<p>The direct precursor to gastrointestinal cancer in patients with PJS remains unknown {16344569}. Peutz&ndash;Jeghers polyps are likely an epiphenomenon to the cancer-prone condition and not obligate malignant precursors. Indeed, dysplasia in a Peutz&ndash;Jeghers polyp is exceedingly rare. In normal colonic crypts from patients with PJS, a protracted clonal evolution has been shown, allowing accumulation of mutations over time, which potentially explains the increased risk of colorectal cancer {16344569; 21940722}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-01-16T16:14:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 375,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 207,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56395,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 243,
        "headingId": 207,
        "headingText": "<p>The macroscopic appearance is not distinctive.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-01-16T16:15:00"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 369,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 169,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 56396,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 243,
        "headingId": 169,
        "headingText": "<p>Colorectal Peutz&ndash;Jeghers polyps have a distinctive histology, with villous architecture and arborizing smooth muscle cores. Epithelial misplacement due to prolapse and peristaltic kneading is relatively common in Peutz&ndash;Jeghers polyps and may extend into the serosa, mimicking a well-differentiated invasive lesion {16344569}. Gastric Peutz&ndash;Jeghers polyps often lack specific histology and are often not readily distinguishable from gastric juvenile polyps or sporadic hyperplastic polyps. Without knowledge of the clinical context, one should therefore be cautious about establishing a new diagnosis of PJS on the basis of gastric polyps alone {20924281; 26721304}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-01-16T16:15:12"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 370,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
